EXT. ABANDONED DRIVE-IN THEATER - SALT FLATS - DUSK

A towering concrete screen, bleached by sun, tilts above rows of dead speaker posts. Wind carries white grit across the lot. In the centre, a battered boxing ring has been assembled from scaffolding and tarp, ropes tied with fishing line.

Three people stand in it.

MARGUERITE "MARGO" DUFRESNE (50s, ex-champion, the shoulders of a woman who once could lift a house) wraps tape around her knuckles. Her trainer, SAUL ABERCROMBIE (70s, hearing aid, lawn chair, stopwatch around his neck), sits ringside. And her young challenger, JOSEFA "JO" TUIASOSOPO (20s, rangy, bruised eyebrow, borrowed gloves) shadowboxes in the corner, quick and angry.

The sun lowers behind the screen. Shadows stretch.

SAUL
Last round.

MARGO
It's the third.

SAUL
You said twelve.

MARGO
I said three if the girl kept up.

Jo spits into a bucket.

JO
I'm keeping up.

MARGO
So far.

Saul clicks the stopwatch. Margo steps forward. Jo meets her at the centre. The bell is a hubcap hit with a wrench.

They circle. Margo is slow, deliberate. Jo throws a jab. Margo slips it, taps her ribs. Jo grunts, throws a hook, misses. Margo pivots and lands a short right. Jo drops to one knee, shakes her head, rises.

SAUL
Seven seconds.

JO
I know.

MARGO
You're dropping your left.

JO
I'm not.

MARGO
You are.

Jo drives forward in a flurry. Margo takes two shots on the gloves, one on the shoulder, catches the third and clinches. They stagger across the ring, locked, panting. Wind whips grit across the tarp. Saul stands, hand on his hearing aid.

SAUL
Break! Break!

They break. Margo wipes sweat. Jo's eye is swelling.

JO
Why are you doing this?

MARGO
Because someone should.

JO
You could have stopped ten years ago.

MARGO
I did stop ten years ago.

JO
Then why come back for me?

Margo does not answer. She lifts her gloves. Jo, after a hesitation, does the same. They touch gloves and step apart.

The sun slips behind the screen. For a moment the concrete glows orange, a vast empty rectangle. Then the light drops, and the lot goes blue.

SAUL
That's the bell.

MARGO
Sun's still up.

SAUL
Sun's down. I looked.

He drops the stopwatch around his neck, eases back into the lawn chair, and takes his hearing aid out. In the ring, Margo and Jo stand with arms at their sides.

JO
Round four?

MARGO
Tomorrow. Come at dawn.

JO
I'll be here.

MARGO
(quietly)
I know you will.

Jo ducks through the ropes and jogs across the lot, her silhouette small against the screen. Margo watches her go. She unwinds the tape slowly from one hand. Saul says nothing. He lights a cigarette and offers her one. She takes it.

They sit, side by side, in the cooling dark.
